    "insights": "# Architectural Insights\n\n## Current State\n- **Pattern:** MVVM detected (0.60 confidence \u2014 moderate)\n- **Scale:** 34 files (small-to-medium project)\n- **Frameworks:** None detected\n\n## Key Observations\n\n**Moderate MVVM confidence (0.60)** suggests the pattern is present but *inconsistently applied*. Common causes:\n- Business logic leaking into Views\n- ViewModels directly referencing Views (breaking separation)\n- Models containing presentation logic\n- Partial adoption across the codebase\n\n**No framework detected** is notable. MVVM typically pairs with a binding mechanism. Without one, you may have manual wiring or roll-your-own infrastructure, which is error-prone.\n\n## Recommendations\n\n### 1. Strengthen Layer Separation\n- Ensure **Views** contain only UI/rendering logic\n- Keep **ViewModels** free of View/UI framework references (testability)\n- Restrict **Models** to data + domain rules\n\n### 2. Formalize the Binding Layer\n- Adopt a binding/observation mechanism (observables, events, or a lightweight reactive library) to reduce manual synchronization bugs.\n\n### 3. Verify Dependency Direction\n```\nView \u2192 ViewModel \u2192 Model\n```\nNo reverse references. ViewModels should never import Views.\n\n### 4. Consistency Audit\nWith only 34 files, a quick manual review can push confidence higher. Identify the \"non-conforming\" files dragging the score down.\n\n### 5. Add Structure for Growth\n- Organize by feature or by layer (`/views`, `/viewmodels`, `/models`, `/services`)\n- Consider a **Services/Repository** layer if data access is currently inside ViewModels\n\n## Quick Wins\n| Action | Impact |\n|--------|--------|\n| Remove View refs from ViewModels | High (testability) |\n| Introduce a binding mechanism | High (maintainability) |\n| Add a services layer | Medium |\n| Standardize folder structure | Medium |\n\n---\n*Note: Confidence is moderate \u2014 these insights are directional. Sharing file/folder structure would enable more specific guidance.*"
